{{!-- One rating as a framed tile — the same device the character sheet gives a stat: a rough-inked
      frame with the rating's name punched into its top rule and its value centred inside. A steading
      rating does the same job as a character stat (a named number you roll), so it reads the same
      way, and someone who knows one sheet already knows this one.

      That frame is also what ends the "which number is the value?" confusion structurally: the name
      sits ON the border and the note sits ON the bottom border, so neither can be mistaken for the
      value between them.

      A NUMERIC rating (Fortunes, Surplus, Population, Prosperity, Defenses) renders the shared
      stepper — `activateSteppers` drives ▲▼ by dispatching a native `change`, which the steading's
      ChangeActionRouter picks up like any other. A NAMED one (Size) renders a select. The snapshot
      decides which; nothing here branches on the slug.

      `.steading-attr-input` is load-bearing: the steadfast item sheet has no change router and binds
      these by that class (StonetopSteadfastSheet._onRender). Renaming it silently breaks editing
      there, so the two must move together.

      Params: attr (slug), attrData (RatingSnapshot), rollable, changeAction (defaults to the generic
      `attribute` handler), panelClass, and optionally advice (an "If you want to…" topic key). --}}
<div class="steading-tile {{panelClass}}" data-attr="{{attr}}">

	{{!-- The book gives Fortunes and Surplus an illustrated arch and the other four a plain rule, and
	      that hierarchy is the thing worth keeping from the printed sheet.

	      The WHOLE PANEL is lifted — outline, illustration and the distressed band at its foot (see
	      scripts/import/pdf/steading-art.js). No frame of our own: a rectangle can never crop a curved
	      shoulder cleanly, so drawing an arch around a cropped interior put two strokes on the page
	      and clipped the rays off Fortunes to do it.

	      Decorative: the rating's name and value are right beneath, so the picture names nothing the
	      reader cannot already read. Absent gracefully — with no art installed the img 404s,
	      HideBrokenImages tags it, and the panel goes with it.

	      The woodcut is a pure-alpha silhouette, and the stylesheet paints it in the season's tint by
	      masking a flat fill with it. The path it masks with is the stylesheet's own (keyed off the
	      rating's class), NOT this one: a relative url() in a custom property resolves against the
	      sheet that uses the variable, so a path handed in from here resolves inside styles/ and
	      404s — and a mask that fails to load takes the whole arch with it. --}}
	{{#if attrData.badge}}
		<span class="steading-arch" aria-hidden="true">
			<img class="steading-tile-badge" src="{{attrData.badge}}" alt="">
		</span>
	{{/if}}

	<span class="steading-tile-label">
		{{!-- Both labels ship, and the SHORT one is decoration only. Where the line has no room for
		      "Prosperity" it shows "Pros", but the full word stays in the DOM — hidden visually, never
		      removed — so what a screen reader announces never shrinks to an abbreviation it would
		      have to decode. --}}
		{{!-- The NAME is the roll button — one control, so there is nothing to keep in step — and the
		      die rides inside it as the visible cue. Without it a name that rolls looks exactly like
		      one that doesn't, which is the same die the character sheet's moves use (move-item.hbs),
		      so a reader who knows one sheet knows this one. It is decoration: the button's accessible
		      name is still "Roll Prosperity". --}}
		{{#if rollable}}
			<button type="button" class="steading-stat-roll rollable" data-roll="{{attr}}"
			        aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.rollRating' rating=attrData.title}}"><span class="steading-title-full">{{attrData.title}}</span><span class="steading-title-short" aria-hidden="true">{{attrData.shortTitle}}</span><i class="fas fa-dice-d6 steading-roll-die" aria-hidden="true"></i></button>
		{{else}}
			<span class="steading-tile-title"><span class="steading-title-full">{{attrData.title}}</span><span class="steading-title-short" aria-hidden="true">{{attrData.shortTitle}}</span></span>
		{{/if}}
		{{#if advice}}{{> "stonetop.advice-button" variant="inline" topic=advice}}{{/if}}
	</span>

	<span class="steading-tile-value">
		{{#if attrData.isNumeric}}
			<span class="stonetop-stepper">
				<input type="number" class="stonetop-step steading-attr-input"
				       data-change-action="{{#if changeAction}}{{changeAction}}{{else}}attribute{{/if}}" data-attr="{{attr}}"
				       value="{{attrData.current}}" step="1"
				       {{#if attrData.hasMin}}min="{{attrData.min}}"{{/if}}
				       {{~#if attrData.hasMax}} max="{{attrData.max}}"{{/if}}
				       aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.ratingValue' rating=attrData.title}}"
				       {{#unless @root.editable}}disabled{{/unless}}>
				{{> "stonetop.stepper-buttons"}}
			</span>
		{{else}}
			<select class="steading-attr-input steading-tile-select"
			        data-change-action="{{#if changeAction}}{{changeAction}}{{else}}attribute{{/if}}" data-attr="{{attr}}"
			        aria-label="{{localize 'stonetop.a11y.ratingValue' rating=attrData.title}}"
			        {{#unless @root.editable}}disabled{{/unless}}>
				{{#each attrData.options}}
					<option value="{{value}}" {{#if selected}}selected{{/if}}>{{label}}</option>
				{{/each}}
			</select>
		{{/if}}
	</span>

	{{!-- Straddles the bottom rule: the one thing this rating most needs to say about its value —
	      a debility's effect, the book's word for the number, a population band, or where it began. --}}
	{{#if (hasText attrData.note)}}
		<span class="steading-tile-note steading-tile-note--{{attrData.noteKind}}">{{attrData.note}}</span>
	{{/if}}

</div>
